1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:02,503
A jumbo jet weighs hundreds of tons.

2
00:00:02,909 --> 00:00:04,419
So how does it stay in the air?

3
00:00:04,684 --> 00:00:06,034
The secret is in the wing.

4
00:00:06,340 --> 00:00:09,150
A wing is curved on top
and slightly flatter below.

5
00:00:09,521 --> 00:00:11,851
And it is tilted a little
upward as it moves.

6
00:00:12,242 --> 00:00:15,432
As the wing cuts through the air,
it pushes the air downward.

7
00:00:15,707 --> 00:00:18,777
When the wing pushes air down,
the air pushes the wing up.

8
00:00:19,003 --> 00:00:22,853
The air above the wing also flows faster,
and so presses less.

9
00:00:23,169 --> 00:00:25,439
That difference in pressure
lifts the wing too.

10
00:00:25,724 --> 00:00:26,694
But there is a catch.

11
00:00:27,085 --> 00:00:30,155
A wing makes lift only when
air is rushing past it.

12
00:00:30,311 --> 00:00:33,141
That is why a plane needs
engines and a long runway.

13
00:00:33,472 --> 00:00:39,042
At about 250 kilometres an hour, the
wings take over, and the plane lifts off.

14
00:00:39,327 --> 00:00:42,277
The first people to do this
were two brothers from Ohio.

15
00:00:42,833 --> 00:00:44,063
Orville and Wilbur Wright.

16
00:00:44,354 --> 00:00:49,864
On 17 December 1903, at Kitty Hawk,
their flyer rose into the air.

17
00:00:50,104 --> 00:00:54,394
It stayed up for just twelve
seconds and flew about 120 feet.

18
00:00:54,685 --> 00:00:56,855
Today, planes cross oceans in hours.

19
00:00:57,201 --> 00:00:59,071
All because of wings, air, and speed.

